[38;5;231m⏺[39m Third question — with no bundler today, how the "other forms" actually get at the id.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the identity module is a real module with an import boundary, or a global. This decides what every
  future consumer's first line looks like, and retrofitting it is a change to every file that touched it.

  - [1mES modules[0m ([38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m, [38;5;153mexport[39m/[38;5;153mimport[39m) — a genuine boundary: [38;5;153mimport { getUserId } from [39m
    [38;5;153m'./auth.js'[39m. Buys encapsulation, no load-order coupling, and a module system that survives adding a bundler later.
    Costs: module scripts won't load over [38;5;153mfile://[39m — you'd need to open the page through a local HTTP server. That's the
    one real tradeoff.
  - [1mGlobal namespace object[0m ([38;5;153mwindow.Auth = {...}[39m in a plain script) — buys zero infrastructure change and keeps [38;5;153mfile://[39m
    working. Costs: load order becomes a real constraint, nothing is private, and every future consumer is coupled to a
    global name. Migrating to modules later touches every consumer.
  - [1mAdd a bundler[0m — solves problems this app doesn't have. YAGNI; I'd skip it.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules.[0m You told me other forms will need this later, and "later consumers" is precisely the
  case where an import boundary pays for itself. The [38;5;153mfile://[39m cost is one [38;5;153mnpx serve[39m-style command, and I'd note it in the
  README as part of this change.
